  "description": "The\u202fPerson entity is identified by the URI\u202fhttp://example.com/person.schema.json and follows the draft\u201107 specification. It is described as \u201cA person object\u201d and is defined as an object type with the title\u202fPerson. This object must contain at least one and no more than five properties, and no additional properties are permitted beyond those explicitly listed.\n\nThe required properties are\u202fname\u202fand\u202fage.\u202fName\u202fmust be a non\u2011empty string (at least one character), while\u202fage\u202fmust be an integer that is zero or greater. An optional\u202femail\u202ffield, if present, must be a string formatted as a valid email address. A\u202ftags\u202ffield, when used, is an array of up to ten unique strings, each entry being a plain string. The schema also includes a reusable definition called\u202fnonEmptyString\u202fwhich enforces the same non\u2011empty string rule. A custom flag\u202fmy_keyword\u202fis set to true, and any numeric value in the structure must be a multiple of\u202f0.5."
